diff --git a/dm/games/README.md b/dm/games/README.md
index 0fbdac3c..025a10a2 100644
--- a/dm/games/README.md
+++ b/dm/games/README.md
@@ -264,9 +264,10 @@ One row per profile; the notes are each profile's `README.md`.
 | `device_api` | Optional, decided by the SDK version without it: the NVN API version `major.minor` that `nvnDeviceGetInteger` reports, 55.15 for SDK 11 and later (what Mario requires) and 53.313 for SDK 10 and older (`hle_nvn.c` `nvn_api`). Games check it and skip creating their device on a mismatch: Super Mario 3D World + Bowser's Fury (SDK 10) requires major 53 and minor ≥ 313. No profile sets it. `DM_NVN_API` overrides. Not part of the shader chain version (only `shader_*` keys are) |
 | `depth_mode` | `gl` (the default, NVN's near = −w) or `zero` (near = 0): the depth mode the game sets with `nvnDeviceSetDepthMode`, for the pipeline bases made before the game runs: the [pipeline archive made offline](../../docs/development.md#the-pipeline-archive-made-offline) for the iPhone (`main0` or `main0_z` vertex entries; built for a game with `pso_mtl4 on` or with `DM_PSO_GPUS`) and the NSP script's Mac prebuild (`DM_PSO_PREBUILD_DEPTH`). The host itself follows the mode the game sets; a wrong value only costs the device its own prebuild. The heap and address region checks of a provisional or derived profile set it when their boot reaches the game's call; without that call they leave it, as no call means unknown, not GL. Keep the key when the profile moves into `dm/games`: it is no shader input. Great Ace Attorney Chronicles, 13 Sentinels (both releases), Big Brain Academy, Link's Awakening, Engage, ASTRAL CHAIN, Luigi's Mansion 3, Octopath Traveler 0 and Kirby Air Riders set `zero` |
 | `render_base` | The 3D views' base size, docked then handheld (`1600x900 1280x720`): what `DM_RENDER_SCALE` scales. `settings.py` writes it into the render scale's row of `launcher.json` as `base`, so a front end can show the setting in pixels. Only for a game with a render scale stub, and only a size its note documents. The slider's top is the stub's largest scale, which the rewriter writes as `RENDER_SCALE` in the fixups table (1.0, or 3.0 for Hades II, whose stub also raises its 1280x720) |
-| `op_mode` | The operation mode the game sees at start: `console` (the default, docked) or `handheld`. Super Mario Galaxy and Captain Toad: Treasure Tracker use `handheld`: docked, their pointer is the Joy-Con gyro, which the host does not have; handheld, it is the touch screen, which the iPhone front end feeds with the touches no gamepad control takes (in the 3D layout, outside the right third of the screen, which is the right stick; `DmGamepad.m`). Handheld also renders at 720p. `DM_OPMODE=0` / `1` overrides, also from `Documents/env.txt` |
+| `op_mode` | The operation mode the game sees at start: `console` (the default, docked) or `handheld`. Super Mario Galaxy and Captain Toad: Treasure Tracker use `handheld`: docked, their pointer is the Joy-Con gyro, which the host does not have; handheld, it is the touch screen, which the iPhone front end feeds with the touches no gamepad control takes where the profile forwards them (the 3D layout forwards where `touch_forward` is set, outside the right third of the screen, which is the right stick; `DmGamepad.m`). Handheld also renders at 720p. `DM_OPMODE=0` / `1` overrides, also from `Documents/env.txt` |
 | `perf_mode` | `follow` (the default): the performance mode follows the operation mode (Boost docked, Normal handheld); `boost`: Boost whatever the operation mode (`hle_oe.c`), a combination a Switch 1 never reports, so a profile choice. Rhythm Heaven Groove plays handheld with Boost: docked means a TV to the game, which then asks for a latency calibration, and only Boost gives its 1920x1080 draw size (handheld Normal draws 1280x720) |
-| `pad_layout` | The iPhone virtual gamepad's layout on first launch: `simple` (the default) = 2D (left stick, A/B/X/Y, shoulders, − / +), `full` = 3D (adds a D-pad, a right stick and L3 / R3; the right stick is not drawn: a touch landing in the right third of the screen on no control holds it, centred where it lands), `touch` = Tap (no control but the layout button: every touch goes to the game's touch screen; needs `op_mode handheld`, Balatro). The layout button at the top centre goes to the next layout in game (Tap only in handheld mode) and the app remembers the player's choice (`DmGamepad.m`) |
+| `pad_layout` | The iPhone virtual gamepad's layout on first launch: `simple` (the default) = 2D (left stick, A/B/X/Y, shoulders, − / +), `full` = 3D (adds a D-pad, a right stick and L3 / R3; the right stick is not drawn: a touch landing in the right third of the screen on no control holds it, centred where it lands), `touch` = Tap (no control but the layout button: every touch goes to the game's touch screen, docked or handheld; Balatro). The in-game menu offers 3D and Tap (2D is no longer a new choice, though a saved 2D preference still applies) and the app remembers the player's choice (`DmGamepad.m`) |
+| `touch_forward` | `on` makes the 3D layout's touches that hit no control go to the game's touch screen; `off` (the default) keeps them out of the game. Tap always forwards. On for the games that need the screen in 3D: Super Mario Galaxy / 2 and Super Mario Sunshine (the pointer), Captain Toad (touch blocks) and Big Brain Academy (its activities). `DM_TOUCH_FORWARD=0` / `1` overrides |
 | `tex_flush_upload` | `on` / `off` (the default): a texture the game fills from the CPU in place (no packaged layout, no `WriteTexels`) and publishes with `nvnMemoryPoolFlushMappedRange` is uploaded from guest memory like a packaged one, and re-uploaded when its pages change (`ft_add` in `hle_nvn.c`). Tears of the Kingdom streams all its textures this way; without it the 3D scene is black. Off by default because a flushed range in another game could cover an image a shader writes, whose GPU content the upload would replace. Without the key, every game still gets a texture's storage once when the game flushed it before the texture's first use (`nvn_flushed` in `hle_nvn.c`: Breath of the Wild's sky tables); the key is for textures the CPU writes again after that |
 | `tex_dirty_upload` | `on` / `off` (the default), needs `tex_flush_upload`: a registered texture whose pages the CPU writes after `nvnTextureInitialize` without any NVN flush is uploaded as well (its pages are write-protected at registration, checked on first use; `nvn_ft_written` in `hle_nvn.c`). Tears of the Kingdom fills its ASTC terrain material arrays this way; without it the cave is magenta. FINAL FANTASY TACTICS - The Ivalice Chronicles loads its UI textures straight into CPU-uncached pools, which need no flush, after creating them; without both keys its screen stays black. The Great Ace Attorney Chronicles copies every texture into a CPU-cached pool after creating it and never flushes (its `main` has no cache maintenance at all): black too. The Legend of Zelda: Ocarina of Time writes its terrain tiles' height, blend and hole maps this way, and without the keys has holes in the ground |
 | `tex_dyn_slots` | `on` / `off` (the default): a shader whose buffer-texture handle the translator could not trace to one slot gets Ryujinx's fallback arrays (`<stage>_<tb|s>_cb0_40000000`: 32 buffer textures and samplers indexed by the handle's texture / sampler ID; any other sampled texture indexes the whole pool without a key, [Texture handles from game memory index the pools](../../docs/reference/shaders.md#texture-handles-from-game-memory-index-the-pools)). With the key the replay puts slot indices in that shader's c2 texture and sampler tables (texture slot r → r, sampler slot r → r << 20) and binds slot k at element k, so the shader's own arithmetic picks the binding (`NVN_RB_TEXD` / `NVN_RB_SAMPD` in `nvn_mtl.m`). Right only where the handle comes from c2's tables: Octopath Traveler 0's 61 such vertex shaders read separate texture slots 0-10 and samplers 0-4 there and nothing else (FINAL FANTASY RESONANCE's 2,077 the texture table words 0x168-0x1c0 and sampler table words 0x568-0x590). Off, such shaders do not load. Not part of the shader chain version |
